     "type": "intro",
     "title": "The Rebel Who Dropped Everything",
     "content": "Imagine a curious, stubborn young man in Renaissance Italy who refuses to take anyone's word for anything. If a professor says heavy things fall faster than light things, he doesn't nod along. He goes and **checks**.\n\nThat man was **Galileo Galilei**, born in Pisa in 1564. He is often called the father of **experimental physics** because he did something radical for his time: instead of arguing about how nature *should* behave, he measured how it *actually* behaves.\n\nIn this lesson you'll live through his story. You'll watch him point a homemade telescope at the night sky and discover moons that no one had ever seen. You'll follow the simple, elegant experiments that revealed a hidden rule of falling objects. You'll meet the equation d = ½ g t² and understand exactly what every symbol means.\n\nAnd you'll see why, nearly 400 years later, an astronaut stood on the Moon and dropped a hammer and a feather to prove Galileo right.\n\nLet's begin where he did: with a question nobody thought to ask.",
