     "type": "concept",
     "title": "A Boy From Pisa Who Wouldn't Sit Still",
     "content": "Galileo was born on February 15, 1564, in **Pisa**, the same Italian city famous for its leaning tower. His father, Vincenzo, was a musician who loved testing ideas about sound with real strings and weights, and some of that hands-on spirit rubbed off.\n\nYoung Galileo was sent to study **medicine** at the University of Pisa, because that's where the money was. But he kept sneaking off to math and physics lectures. He found numbers and motion far more thrilling than memorizing anatomy, and he never finished the medical degree.\n\nOne famous story says that as a student, he sat in the Pisa cathedral watching a lamp swing back and forth on its chain. Using his own **pulse** as a clock, he noticed the swing took the same amount of time whether it swung far or barely at all. That observation about **pendulums** would echo through his life's work.\n\nHe was sharp-tongued, confident, and allergic to accepting things \"because a wise old author said so.\" This personality made him brilliant and also, eventually, dangerous to the powerful people whose ideas he challenged.\n\nBy his mid-twenties he was teaching mathematics, already convinced that the universe was written in a language he intended to read.",
