    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "The Seed of Inertia",
     "content": "While studying falling, Galileo stumbled onto an even deeper idea. Roll a ball down one ramp and up another facing it, and the ball climbs back up almost to the height it started from. Make the second ramp shallower, and the ball rolls **farther** to reach that same height.\n\nSo Galileo asked a beautiful \"what if.\" What if the second ramp were perfectly flat, never rising at all? The ball, always trying to reach its old height, would roll **forever**, never finding a reason to stop.\n\nThis is the seed of what we now call **inertia**: an object in motion stays in motion, in a straight line, unless something like friction or a wall stops it. It flatly contradicts everyday intuition, where things always seem to slow down and halt. Galileo realized they only stop because of hidden forces like friction and air, not because stopping is their natural state.\n\nThis was revolutionary. It meant rest and steady motion are really the same peaceful condition, just seen from different points of view. Decades later, **Isaac Newton** would make this Galileo's insight the first of his three laws of motion, building the whole of classical physics on top of it.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "caption": "A ball seeks its old height; flatten the far ramp and it would roll forever.",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Birth of Inertia</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What if the ramp never rose again?</text><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"420\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"420\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"220\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"235\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#f59e0b\"/><circle cx=\"580\" cy=\"235\" r=\"16\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.4)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"360\" y=\"480\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">climbs back to the same height</text><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"620\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"820\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"820\" x2=\"1000\" y2=\"820\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"635\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#f59e0b\"/><circle cx=\"700\" cy=\"800\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#10b981\"/><text x=\"740\" y=\"770\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">-></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"890\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">flat ramp: never finds its height</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"940\" width=\"800\" height=\"120\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"990\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Motion continues unless something stops it.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1030\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Newton would later call this the first law.</text></svg>"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Turning a Toy Into a Telescope",
     "content": "In 1609, Galileo heard that Dutch spectacle-makers had built a gadget that made faraway things look closer. He never saw one, but from the description he **built his own** within days, then kept grinding better lenses until his telescope magnified about 20 times, far beyond the flimsy originals.\n\nThen he did something almost nobody had bothered to do carefully: he pointed it at the **night sky**.\n\nWhat he saw shattered the old picture of a perfect, unchanging heaven. The Moon wasn't a smooth crystal ball, it had **mountains and craters**, with shadows that shifted as the sunlight angle changed, just like landscapes on Earth. The hazy Milky Way dissolved into countless individual stars.\n\nMost stunning of all, in January 1610 he spotted four tiny points of light beside **Jupiter**. Night after night they danced from side to side, and he realized they were **moons orbiting Jupiter**, not Earth. Here was proof that not everything circled our planet.\n\nHe published these discoveries in a short, electrifying book called *Sidereus Nuncius*, the \"Starry Messenger.\" Overnight, Galileo became the most famous scientist in Europe, and the trouble of his life began.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "caption": "Through a homemade telescope: craters on the Moon and four moons circling Jupiter.",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Starry Messenger, 1610</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What Galileo saw in the sky</text><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"320\" r=\"130\" fill=\"rgba(136,136,136,0.15)\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"290\" r=\"22\" fill=\"rgba(136,136,136,0.3)\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"350\" r=\"30\" fill=\"rgba(136,136,136,0.3)\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"270\" r=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(136,136,136,0.3)\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"500\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Moon has craters</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"533\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">not a perfect sphere</text><circle cx=\"800\" cy=\"320\" r=\"70\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.2)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"330\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jupiter</text><circle cx=\"640\" cy=\"320\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#ffd700\"/><circle cx=\"700\" cy=\"320\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#ffd700\"/><circle cx=\"900\" cy=\"320\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#ffd700\"/><circle cx=\"960\" cy=\"320\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#ffd700\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"500\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Four moons orbit Jupiter</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"533\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">not everything circles Earth</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"620\" width=\"800\" height=\"180\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"675\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" text-anchor=\"middle\">He also saw Venus show phases</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"720\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">like the Moon, which only fits a</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"754\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sun-centered solar system.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"900\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The heavens were not perfect or unchanging.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"960\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Overnight, Galileo became Europe's star.</text></svg>"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "The Trial That Silenced Him",
     "content": "Galileo's telescope observations convinced him that **Copernicus** was right: the Earth and planets orbit the **Sun**, and the Earth is not the fixed center of everything. Venus showing a full cycle of phases, like the Moon, only made sense if it circled the Sun.\n\nThe Catholic Church, however, held that the Earth stood still at the center, and it read certain Bible passages that way. In 1616 Galileo was warned to stop teaching the Sun-centered idea as fact.\n\nHe kept quiet for years, then in 1632 published his masterpiece, the *Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems*. It staged a debate between characters, but the one defending the old Earth-centered view, named **Simplicio**, came across as a bit of a fool. The Pope, once friendly to Galileo, felt mocked.\n\nIn **1633**, at nearly 70, Galileo was summoned before the **Inquisition** in Rome. Under threat, he formally took back his claim that the Earth moves. Legend says he muttered \"*e pur si muove*,\" \"and yet it moves,\" though there's no solid proof he actually said it.\n\nHe was sentenced to **house arrest** for the rest of his life. His great book was banned.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "caption": "Two rival pictures of the cosmos, and the 1633 trial that forced Galileo to recant.",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Two World Systems, One Trial</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Why 1633 changed his life</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"160\" width=\"440\" height=\"360\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ef4444\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Old view: Earth center</text><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"360\" r=\"30\" fill=\"#3b82f6\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"367\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Earth</text><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"360\" r=\"120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"240\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#ffd700\"/><rect x=\"580\" y=\"160\" width=\"440\" height=\"360\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Copernicus: Sun center</text><circle cx=\"800\" cy=\"360\" r=\"26\" fill=\"#ffd700\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"367\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sun</text><circle cx=\"800\" cy=\"360\" r=\"120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"800\" cy=\"240\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#3b82f6\"/><rect x=\"200\" y=\"580\" width=\"700\" height=\"360\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"635\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1633: The Inquisition</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"695\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Galileo, nearly 70, is forced</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"729\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">to take back his words.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"785\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sentence: house arrest for life.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"845\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Legend: \"e pur si muove\"</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"879\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\"and yet it moves\"</text></svg>"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quote",
     "content": "\"Philosophy is written in this grand book, the universe ... It is written in the language of mathematics.\"\n\nGalileo wrote this in his 1623 book *The Assayer*. In one sentence it captures his whole revolution. He was saying that nature isn't understood through clever word-arguments alone, but by **measuring** things and describing them with numbers, geometry, and equations.\n\nThat belief, that the world runs on rules you can write down and test, is the foundation every scientist and engineer still stands on today. When you use d = ½ g t² to predict where a falling object lands, you are reading a page of Galileo's grand book.",
     "author": "Galileo Galilei, The Assayer (1623)",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "caption": "Galileo's conviction: nature is written in the language of mathematics.",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Language of Nature</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">From The Assayer, 1623</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"300\" width=\"860\" height=\"360\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"380\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\"The universe is written</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"430\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">in the language</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"480\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">of mathematics.\"</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"580\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Measure, then describe with</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"614\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">numbers and equations.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"770\" font-size=\"40\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">d = ½ g t²</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"860\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">One line of that grand book,</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"900\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">still true today.</text></svg>"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Blindness, Arcetri, and a Lasting Gift",
     "content": "House arrest was spent at his villa in **Arcetri**, in the hills above Florence. Isolated and watched, the old man refused to stop working. He turned away from the dangerous topic of the heavens and back to the safe, solid ground of **motion**.\n\nThere he wrote his greatest scientific book, *Two New Sciences*, which was smuggled out and printed in Protestant Holland in 1638, beyond the Church's reach. In it he laid out his laws of falling bodies, uniform acceleration, and the strength of materials, the true birth certificate of physics as a science.\n\nHeartbreakingly, by the time it appeared he had gone almost completely **blind**, probably from years of eye strain and illness. The man who had shown humanity new worlds in the sky could no longer see the stars at all. He is supposed to have said the heavens he had opened were now shut to him alone.\n\nGalileo died at Arcetri on **January 8, 1642**, still under house arrest. Yet his method survived him. In 1992, 350 years later, the Catholic Church formally acknowledged that he had been right. His real monument is every experiment ever run since.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "caption": "Galileo's final years at Arcetri: blind, confined, yet writing the founding book of physics.",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Final Years</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Arcetri, 1633-1642</text><line x1=\"550\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"880\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"230\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#ef4444\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"190\" width=\"370\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"305\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1633: House arrest begins</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">confined at his villa</text><circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"410\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#10b981\"/><rect x=\"610\" y=\"370\" width=\"390\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"805\" y=\"410\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1638: Two New Sciences</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"442\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">smuggled out and printed</text><circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"590\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#f59e0b\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"550\" width=\"370\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"305\" y=\"590\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Goes almost fully blind</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"622\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the sky-watcher cannot see</text><circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"770\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\"/><rect x=\"610\" y=\"730\" width=\"390\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"805\" y=\"770\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jan 8, 1642: dies</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"802\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">still under house arrest</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"960\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1992: the Church admits he was right.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1015\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">His method outlived every accusation.</text></svg>"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "A Hammer and a Feather on the Moon",
     "content": "Galileo could never fully prove his boldest claim, that a heavy object and a light one fall at exactly the same rate, because on Earth the air always cheats. Drop a hammer and a feather in your kitchen and the feather flutters down slowly, seeming to confirm Aristotle.\n\nBut Galileo insisted: it's only the **air**. Remove the air, and they must land together.\n\nOn August 2, 1971, an astronaut finally staged the perfect test. Standing on the **Moon**, where there is essentially no air, Apollo 15 commander **David Scott** held out a geologist's **hammer** in one hand and a **falcon feather** in the other. On live television, he let go of both at the same instant.\n\nThey fell side by side and struck the lunar dust at exactly the same moment. Scott grinned and said, \"How about that? Mr. Galileo was correct.\"\n\nIt was a 4-billion-dollar demonstration of a 350-year-old idea, performed a quarter-million miles from Earth. With the air removed, weight truly stopped mattering. The heavy hammer and the wispy feather obeyed the same simple rule Galileo had chased down a wooden ramp in Padua.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "caption": "Apollo 15, 1971: with no air on the Moon, hammer and feather hit the ground together.",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hammer and Feather</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Apollo 15 on the Moon, 1971</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"170\" width=\"440\" height=\"720\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"215\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ef4444\" text-anchor=\"middle\">On Earth (air)</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"250\" width=\"40\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#f59e0b\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"800\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#f59e0b\"/><text x=\"170\" y=\"330\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">hammer</text><path d=\"M400 250 q-30 60 10 120 q-30 60 10 120 q-30 60 10 120\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"410\" y=\"640\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">feather</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"860\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">feather drifts, lands late</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"170\" width=\"440\" height=\"720\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"215\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">On the Moon (no air)</text><rect x=\"680\" y=\"250\" width=\"40\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#f59e0b\"/><rect x=\"880\" y=\"250\" width=\"24\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#3b82f6\"/><line x1=\"700\" y1=\"290\" x2=\"700\" y2=\"800\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 6\"/><line x1=\"892\" y1=\"258\" x2=\"892\" y2=\"800\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 6\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"800\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#f59e0b\"/><rect x=\"880\" y=\"800\" width=\"24\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#3b82f6\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"860\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">both land together</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"960\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\"Mr. Galileo was correct.\"</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1020\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Remove the air and weight stops mattering.</text></svg>"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "application",
     "title": "Galileo in Your Everyday World",
     "content": "Galileo's rules aren't dusty history, they run silently through your daily life.\n\n• **Car airbags** fire in about 30 milliseconds because engineers use equations of motion, direct descendants of d = ½ g t², to predict exactly how fast your body flies forward in a crash and when the bag must inflate.\n• **Video games and movie effects** look believable because their physics engines calculate falling and jumping using the very same acceleration formula. When a character leaps and arcs back down, the code is quietly running Galileo's math frame by frame.\n• **Spacecraft and rovers** are landed by computers that model gravity's steady pull with these equations, timing engine burns to the fraction of a second.\n• **Amusement park rides** and even the sensors in your **phone** that detect a fall rely on the fact that acceleration is predictable.\n\nMore than any single formula, Galileo left you a **habit of mind**: don't just accept what an authority claims, design a test and let reality answer. Every time you check a fact for yourself instead of taking it on faith, you're using the tool Galileo forged. That skeptical, evidence-first attitude is his true legacy, and it's available to you every single day.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "caption": "From airbags to games to spacecraft, Galileo's motion rules run through modern life.",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Galileo All Around You</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Where his physics shows up today</text><circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"330\" r=\"120\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"320\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">d = ½ g t²</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"360\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">motion rules</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"530\" width=\"480\" height=\"110\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"575\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#6366f1\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Car airbags</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"610\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">timed by equations of motion</text><rect x=\"560\" y=\"530\" width=\"480\" height=\"110\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"575\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Game physics engines</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"610\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">jumps and falls, frame by frame</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"670\" width=\"480\" height=\"110\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"715\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Spacecraft landings</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"750\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">engine burns to the split second</text><rect x=\"560\" y=\"670\" width=\"480\" height=\"110\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(236,72,153,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"715\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#ec4899\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Phone fall sensors</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"750\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">detect a drop before impact</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"850\" width=\"800\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"905\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">His biggest gift: test it yourself.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"955\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Don't take claims on faith. Check them.</text></svg>"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "According to Galileo, why does a feather fall more slowly than a hammer on Earth but not on the Moon?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "The Moon's gravity makes light objects heavier",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "On Earth the air resists the feather; the Moon has essentially no air, so both fall at the same rate",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "Feathers are attracted less strongly by Earth than by the Moon",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "The hammer creates a vacuum that pulls the feather down faster",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "Galileo argued that all objects accelerate downward at the same rate, and only air resistance makes light, fluffy objects seem to fall slower. On the Moon there is essentially no air to interfere, so Apollo 15's hammer and feather fell together and hit the surface at the same instant, exactly as Galileo predicted more than three centuries earlier.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "caption": "Air resistance, not weight, explains the feather's slow fall on Earth.",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">It Was the Air All Along</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Weight was never the cause</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"200\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"320\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#ef4444\" text-anchor=\"middle\">With air</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"320\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Air pushes up on the</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"356\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">wide, light feather.</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"420\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Feather lags behind.</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"200\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"780\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"23\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">No air (vacuum)</text><text x=\"780\" y=\"320\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Nothing resists them.</text><text x=\"780\" y=\"356\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Both accelerate at g.</text><text x=\"780\" y=\"420\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">They land together.</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"600\" width=\"700\" height=\"240\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"670\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Same rule for both objects:</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"730\" font-size=\"34\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">d = ½ g t²</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"800\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">no weight term anywhere in it</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"930\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Remove the air and Aristotle disappears.</text></svg>"
     }
    }
